(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-inflammation-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Inflammation — Quiz 2","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which three cell types does the lecture list as granulocytes?","choices":["Lymphocytes, monocytes and macrophages","Neutrophils, basophils and eosinophils","Neutrophils, monocytes and B lymphocytes","Basophils, eosinophils and natural killer cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the three granulocytes; polymorphonuclear leukocyte refers to all granulocytes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The term agranulocyte is described as somewhat misleading. Why?","choices":["Because these cells acquire granules only after activation","Because their granules are visible only in chronic inflammation","Because these cells do contain non-specific azurophilic granules — lysosomes","Because they are actually a subtype of polymorphonuclear leukocyte"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The name implies an absence of granules, but non-specific azurophilic granules (lysosomes) are present. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the neutrophil's nucleus and cytoplasm?","choices":["Bi-lobed nucleus with pink-orange granules","Kidney-shaped nucleus with abundant cytoplasm that is usually agranulated when it is viewed on light microscopy","Multilobed nucleus that may appear like multiple nuclei, with cytoplasm looking transparent because of fine pale lilac granules","Bi- or tri-lobed nucleus with a number of coarse granules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both the multilobed nucleus and the fine pale lilac granules are given. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is described as the first responder to microbial infection?","choices":["The macrophage","The eosinophil","The B lymphocyte","The neutrophil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil is named as the first responder to microbial infection, defending against bacterial and fungal infection. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The lecture contrasts trauma with a bee sting to illustrate which distinction within acute inflammation?","choices":["Damage that is purely physical versus damage that involves activation of an immune response","Inflammation that resolves completely versus inflammation that becomes chronic","Serous versus fibrinous exudate","Endogenous versus exogenous toxic agents"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both produce acute inflammation, but only one recruits an immune response, which is the point of the pairing. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which pair of conditions does the lecture give as examples of autoimmunity causing chronic inflammation?","choices":["Atherosclerosis and silicosis","Asthma and hay fever","Trauma and bee sting","Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both are named as autoimmune causes of chronic inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"The lecture defines a biomaterial in this context as which of the following?","choices":["Any tissue harvested from the patient's own body","Any protein produced during the inflammatory response","Anything not human that is put into a human","Any material that has been sterilised before implantation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is the working definition given, and it is why the opsonisation point generalises across implanted devices. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which property distinguishes the monocyte from the neutrophil in terms of sustained phagocytic capacity?","choices":["The monocyte has a multilobed nucleus permitting repeated division","The monocyte can replace its lysosomal contents","The monocyte does not require opsonisation of its targets","The monocyte carries T cell receptors for direct antigen binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil cannot renew its lysosomes and dies after a few pathogens; the monocyte can replace its lysosomal contents and so keeps working. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In the reviewed sequence of acute inflammation, which vascular event comes FIRST?","choices":["Vasoconstriction","Vasodilation","Increased vascular permeability","Haemoconcentration and stasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sequence opens with vasoconstriction, followed by vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, then haemoconcentration and stasis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The lecture distinguishes endogenous from exogenous toxic agents in chronic inflammation. Which pairing is correct?","choices":["Endogenous — silica; exogenous — atherosclerosis","Endogenous — rheumatoid arthritis; exogenous — lupus","Endogenous — atherosclerosis; exogenous — particulates such as silica","Endogenous — bacterial infection; exogenous — viral infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture uses atherosclerosis as the endogenous example and particulates such as silica as the exogenous one. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Eosinophils primarily deal with which category of infection, and predominate in which reactions?","choices":["Bacterial infection; predominating in suppurative inflammation","Parasitic infection; predominating in allergic reactions such as asthma, hay fever and hives","Viral infection; predominating in cell-mediated immunity","Fungal infection; predominating in chronic granulomatous inflammatory processes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs parasitic infection with predominance in allergic reactions, naming those three examples. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How do phagocytic cells recognise immunoglobulin G bound to foreign material?","choices":["Through the complement receptors that bind to the C3b fragment","Through T cell receptors on the phagocyte surface","Through histamine receptors on the endothelium","Through Fc receptors that recognise the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages and neutrophils carry Fc receptors that bind the Fc portion of the antibody. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Over what timescale does acute inflammation occur, and when does it begin?","choices":["Over days to months, beginning once macrophages arrive","Over weeks, beginning after antibody production starts","Over minutes to hours only, beginning after a full 24 hours have elapsed since the injury","Over seconds, minutes, hours or days, beginning within seconds of tissue injury"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute inflammation begins immediately — within seconds — following injury of tissues. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which vascular change is directly responsible for allowing fluid and blood proteins to enter the interstitium?","choices":["Vasodilation of the supplying arterioles","Vasoconstriction of the draining venules","Endothelial contraction increasing capillary permeability","Haemoconcentration occurring within the capillary bed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endothelial contraction is what allows the increased permeability through which exudation occurs. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Histamine is described as which mediator in the inflammatory response, with what effects?","choices":["The final mediator released during the whole response, causing fibrosis and the formation of dense scar tissue at the site","The first mediator of the initial inflammatory response, causing dilation of arterioles and increased permeability of capillaries and venules","A chemotactic factor drawing neutrophils to the site","An opsonin that coats foreign material for recognition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both the primacy and the two vascular effects are given. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What proportion of circulating serum proteins do complement components represent, and what are their listed actions?","choices":["10%; chemotactic to neutrophils and monocytes, with the cascade able to damage bacteria","50%; primarily responsible for oncotic pressure","1%; acting solely as opsonins","25%; responsible for the formation of a clot at the site of the injury or lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three points are given together. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Through which two routes can the inflammatory process heal an injury or implant site?","choices":["Vasoconstriction of the supplying arterioles, or lymphatic drainage of the exudate","Generation of new tissue by native parenchymal cells, or formation of fibroblastic scar tissue","Phagocytosis by neutrophils alone, or opsonisation by immunoglobulin G","Hemoconcentration followed by stasis, or leukocyte transmigration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly these two outcomes for the sequence of events that heals the site. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which set of functions does the lecture assign to B lymphocytes?","choices":["Cell-mediated killing of virus-infected cells through T cell receptors","Returning immune function to its normal operation after infection, and preventing autoimmunity","Releasing histamine to increase vascular permeability","Making antibodies against antigens, acting as antigen-presenting cells, and developing into memory B cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three are listed, and the B lymphocyte is described as playing a large role in the humoral immune response and being essential to adaptive immunity. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which mediator is distinguished from histamine and serotonin by also producing pain?","choices":["Complement component C3b","Immunoglobulin G","Fibrinogen","Bradykinin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three raise permeability, but pain is listed only for bradykinin — along with a possible increase in leukocyte chemotaxis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Recognition and attachment during phagocytosis are enhanced by the presence of what?","choices":["Serum factors known as opsonins","Histamine released from basophils","Fibrinogen deposited by the coagulation system","Bradykinin generated from plasma proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The review names opsonins explicitly, with immunoglobulin G and complement fragment C3b as the examples. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which combination of effects does bradykinin produce?","choices":["Vasoconstriction, reduced capillary permeability, and analgesia at the site","Antibody production and antigen presentation","Coagulation and trapping of exudate","Increased capillary permeability, pain, and possibly increased leukocyte chemotaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bradykinin is the plasma-protein mediator that adds pain to the permeability effect. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which description matches the serous pattern of inflammation?","choices":["Largely plasma with low protein content, occurring early or with mild inflammation","Large amounts of fibrinogen forming a thick meshwork","Remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris","Mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Serous inflammation is largely plasma, low in protein, and occurs early or when inflammation is mild. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Beyond phagocytosis, what is the monocyte's role in the immune response?","choices":["Secreting histamine to open the capillary bed","Producing the fibrous network that traps exudate","Presenting pieces of pathogens to T cells so the pathogen may be recognised again and killed","Restoring the immune system to its normal function once the infection has resolved"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This antigen-presenting role is what links the monocyte to later, faster responses against the same organism. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which cells does the lecture group as agranulocytes (mononuclear leucocytes)?","choices":["Neutrophils, eosinophils and basophils","Erythrocytes, platelets and circulating plasma cells","Basophils, mast cells and macrophages","Lymphocytes (B cells and T cells), monocytes and macrophages"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These three are grouped as the agranulocytes or mononuclear leucocytes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which statement about neutrophils matches the lecture?","choices":["They are the predominant inflammatory cell in allergic reactions and in parasitic infection","They are first responders to microbial infection, and their activity and death in large numbers forms pus","They mature in the thymus and carry T cell receptors","They present antigen to T cells after phagocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both properties are given, and the second explains the composition of the suppurative pattern. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Digestion of foreign material by activated neutrophils and macrophages involves which four steps?","choices":["Vasodilation, exudation, stasis, and repair","Adhesion, transmigration, aggregation, and chemotaxis","Recognition, attachment, engulfment, and degradation","Opsonisation, complement fixation, lysis, and clearance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these four, and notes that recognition and attachment are enhanced when opsonins are present. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In chronic inflammation, what roles do lymphocytes and plasma cells versus macrophages play?","choices":["Lymphocytes phagocytose the cellular debris at the site, while macrophages produce and secrete the antibody","Both populations act solely to proliferate blood vessels","Lymphocytes and plasma cells mediate antibody production; macrophages process and deliver antigen to immunocompetent cells","Lymphocytes release histamine; macrophages release serotonin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture assigns antibody production to lymphocytes and plasma cells, and antigen processing and delivery to macrophages. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Where do T lymphocytes mature, and what surface structure do they carry?","choices":["The thymus; T cell receptors","The bone marrow; Fc receptors","The spleen; complement receptors","The lymph nodes; major histocompatibility complexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture makes the mnemonic explicit — matures in the Thymus, hence T lymphocyte — and names the T cell receptor. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Ulcerative inflammation is characterised by which change?","choices":["Necrosis and sloughing of surface epithelium, exposing underlying connective tissue","Formation of a membrane-like covering over intact and otherwise viable mucosa","Damage to blood vessels occurring with other exudates","Coagulative necrosis of an entire limb segment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That exposure of the underlying connective tissue is what defines the ulcerative pattern. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which three causes of chronic inflammation does the lecture list?","choices":["Trauma, thermal injury, and radiation exposure","Persistent infection, prolonged exposure to potentially toxic agents, and autoimmunity","Vasodilation, increased permeability, and exudation","Opsonisation of foreign material, phagocytosis by neutrophils, and enzymatic degradation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the three given, with rheumatoid arthritis and lupus offered as the autoimmune examples. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"}]};
